<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Love song · Lament · 1879 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Red River Valley</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>An old cowboy song in which someone says a sad goodbye to a loved one leaving the valley of the Red River.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>settlers and cowboys of Canada and the United States</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>the valley of the northern Red River, Manitoba, Canada, and Minnesota and North Dakota, United States (exact origin unknown)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>known by the 1880s; first printed 1887</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Love song · Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional; words first printed 1887 (manuscript dated 1879 and 1885))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Red River Valley&quot; is a folk song and cowboy standard of uncertain origin, sung under many names, among them &quot;Cowboy Love <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>&quot;, &quot;Bright Sherman Valley&quot;, &quot;In the Bright <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> Valley&quot; and &quot;Bright Little Valley&quot;, depending on where it was sung. Its first known appearance in print was in the Milwaukee paper The Weekly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a> in 1887: on 25 June the paper listed it among songs whose words readers had asked for, and on 17 September it printed a version sent in by a reader from Mondovi, in western Wisconsin. The earliest known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of the words, titled &quot;The Red River Valley&quot;, bears the notes &quot;Nemaha 1879&quot; and &quot;Harlan 1885&quot;, names of counties in Nebraska and of towns in Iowa.</p><p>The Canadian folklorist Edith Fowke found anecdotal evidence that the song was known in at least five Canadian provinces before 1896, which led to the idea that it was made at the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-river-expedition-1870">Wolseley Expedition</a> of 1870 to the Red River Valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba">Manitoba</a>, as the lament of a local woman, perhaps a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a>, whose soldier lover was about to return east. That evidence is sparse and doubtful, and the link to the expedition is almost entirely unsupported. The valley runs south into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, where the river forms the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minnesota">Minnesota</a> and North Dakota, and where exactly the song began is unresolved; but the weight of the evidence shows that it is this northern Red River the song means, not a southern or western river of the same name.</p><p>The song was printed as sheet music in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in 1896, as &quot;In the Bright <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> Valley&quot; by James J. Kerrigan, and Carl Sandburg included words and tune in his American Songbag of 1927. The same year Hugh Cross and Riley Puckett recorded it for Columbia under the title &quot;Red River Valley&quot;, the first commercial recording under its best-known name and the model for many that followed. Gene Autry sang it in the 1936 film Red River Valley, and in John Ford's The Grapes of Wrath (1940), a story of displaced Oklahomans, it became linked with the southern Red River. Members of the Western Writers of America ranked it tenth among the Top 100 Western songs of all time.</p><p>Its tune has carried many other words. &quot;Jarama Valley&quot;, a song about the Battle of Jarama in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>, used its tune and was recorded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woody-guthrie">Woody Guthrie</a> and the Almanac Singers with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pete-seeger">Pete Seeger</a>. Johnny and the Hurricanes turned it into the rock and roll hit &quot;Red River Rock&quot; in 1959, Willie Nelson set new words to it as &quot;Can I Sleep in Your Arms&quot; in 1975, and Helena Vondráčková made her recording debut with a Czech version, &quot;Červená řeka&quot;, in 1964. It is the official slow march of the Fort Garry Horse, a reserve cavalry regiment of the Canadian Army, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> supporters sing the chant &quot;Scouser Tommy&quot; to it.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of parting: the singer begs a loved one who is leaving the valley to stay a while, or at least to remember the valley and the one left behind who loved them truly. The mood is tender and sorrowful.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are in the public domain, but no free, reliable edition has been found yet to import them from, and the Institute never types a song&#8217;s words from memory: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius"><div class="sg-genius-btns"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn" data-genius="982403" data-what="Lyrics">Show the lyrics from Genius</button></div><div class="sg-genius-frames"></div><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press a button. <a href="https://genius.com/Traditional-transcriptions-red-river-valley-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a>.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="sNo7Wm92es8" data-title="Red River Valley: Hugh Cross and Riley Puckett, Columbia 15206-D, recorded 1927: the first commercial recording under the title; sung with guitar"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Hugh Cross and Riley Puckett, Columbia 15206-D, recorded 1927: the first commercial recording under the title; sung with guitar</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Riley Puckett - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sNo7Wm92es8" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">In the Bright Mohawk Valley (James J. Kerrigan)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1896</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sheet music version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Red River Valley (Hugh Cross and Riley Puckett, Columbia 15206-D)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1927</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Jarama Valley</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words, same tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Red River Rock (Johnny and the Hurricanes)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1959</span><span class="sg-chain-h">instrumental rock and roll version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">I'm a Lapp (Sven-Gösta Jonsson)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1959</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Drownin' My Sorrows (Connie Francis)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1963</span><span class="sg-chain-h">tune used for the verses</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Červená řeka (Helena Vondráčková)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1964</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Czech version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Please Don't Play Red River Valley (Johnny Cash)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1966</span><span class="sg-chain-h">humorous song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Can I Sleep in Your Arms (Willie Nelson)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1975</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words, same tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Red River Shore (Bob Dylan)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1997</span><span class="sg-chain-h">motifs and themes borrowed</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Scouser Tommy</span><span class="sg-chain-h">football chant, same tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">New Vegas Valley (Fallout: New Vegas)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2010</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted words and tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Mitnoe'a Ha-Sappan</span><span class="sg-chain-h">early Hebrew version, same melody</span></li></ul></section>
